<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hechingen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hechingen is a town in the Zollernalb district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the foot of the hill crowned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenzollern-castle">Hohenzollern Castle</a>. It was the seat of the Swabian counts of Zollern and, from 1576, of the Hohenzollern-Hechingen line, raised to princely rank in 1623, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> court was known for its music. The town passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1850.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hechingen.jpg" alt="Hechingen" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Muesse · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Klosterkirche_St.Luzen_(Hechingen)_D%C3%A4cher.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hechingen lies in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, about 60 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> and 90 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>. The town stands at the foot of the Swabian Alps below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenzollern-castle">Hohenzollern Castle</a>. Research suggests that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solicinium">Battle of Solicinium</a>, fought in 368 between the invading Alamanni and a Roman army led by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a>, probably took place in what is now the north of Hechingen, and that the lost city of Solicinium stood where the town's Roman museum is today.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Counts of Hohenzollern founded the town in 1255 as their new capital. It lay on an imperial highway running from the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> south by way of Rottweil to the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the Alpine passes. The counts grew steadily weaker through financial problems. After a siege in 1388, Eberhard II, Duke of Württemberg gained feudal rights over the territory, and a fire destroyed the town in 1401. Friedrich XII of Hohenzollern sold his whole fortune to Württemberg in 1415, and in 1423 the castle was taken and destroyed. His nephew Jos Niklaus later won imperial permission to rebuild it and cancelled the agreement with Württemberg, so Hechingen stayed Hohenzollern, though provincial and largely agricultural.</p><p>When the county was divided in three, Hechingen became the residence of the counts of Hohenzollern-Hechingen, whose territory of the town and 26 villages changed little until the 19th century. Count Eitel Friedrich IV made it a centre of art, music and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> architecture, building the convent church of St Luzen and the Friedrichsburg palace. The town stayed Catholic through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> but suffered in the 30 Years' War, with imperial, Swedish, Württemberg and Bavarian troops, plague in 1635 and a complete plundering in 1638 by the troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-of-saxe-weimar">Bernard of Saxe-Weimar</a>. Bavarian troops stayed until 1650.</p><p>Constantine, the last reigning prince, married Eugénie de Beauharnais, and from 1826 the couple made Hechingen a cultural centre of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, receiving guests such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hector-berlioz">Hector Berlioz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a>. After the Revolution of 1848 the county passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1850. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> industries and parts of the Kaiser Wilhelm Society, including its Institute for Physics, were moved to Hechingen from bombed cities, and in April 1945 American troops of the Alsos Mission entered the town in search of material for the German nuclear energy project.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hechingen belongs to the Zollernalbkreis, which absorbed its district in 1973, and is twinned with Joué-lès-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Hódmezővásárhely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Textile and machine-making industries, many of them Jewish enterprises, had made it one of the region's important economic centres by 1871.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hechingen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their castle stood on the steep Zollern hill, an outlier of the Swabian Jura above the town of <b>Hechingen</b>, and from it the family built up rights over lands and churches between the upper Neckar and the Danube.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1576 division of the County of Hohenzollern gave the eldest son, Eitel Frederick IV, the ancestral Hohenzollern castle and the town of <b>Hechingen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hohenzollern-hechingen">Principality of Hohenzollern-Hechingen</a></li></ul></section>
